<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Sanitizing NORLANT, 1962</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;During the Cuban Missile Crisis, the United States Navy's Atlantic Fleet&amp;nbsp;was fully engaged in creating and enforcing the "quarantine" ordered by President Kennedy with few resources to spare for the defence of the coast north of Bermuda.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Although Cuba dominated American naval operations there were also an unknown number of Soviet patrol submarines of the &lt;EM&gt;Zulu&lt;/EM&gt; SS type and ballistic missile submarines of the &lt;EM&gt;Golf&lt;/EM&gt; SSB type believed to be operating in the northwest Atlantic. On 17 October, the&amp;nbsp;Anti-Submarine Warfare commander&amp;nbsp;of the Atlantic Fleet&amp;nbsp;flew from his headquarters at Norfolk, VA to Halifax, Nova Scotia to get Royal Canadian Navy support to hunt for the Soviet boats between the Azores and the Eastern Seaboard. Of particular importance were the long-range, high-endurance CP-107 Argus of the Royal Canadian Air Force (but under operational control of the RCN) since the maritime patrol squadrons equipped with the P-3 Orions now have other duties supporting the anticipated quarantine.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Canada's Prime Minister John Diefenbaker and President John F. Kennedy had very poor personal relations and it would be no exaggeration to state that&amp;nbsp;each loathed the other. However, Kennedy sent&amp;nbsp;one of the best men from the State Department to brief the PM and get Canada's full support for his Cuba policies&amp;nbsp;and to answer any Canadian concerns. Defence Minister Douglas Harkness and Foreign Affairs Minister Howard Green&amp;nbsp;wanted Canada to match the increasing US alert status and provide all available military and naval support as requested but the Prime Minister had other ideas. Diefenbaker&amp;nbsp;thought that Kennedy was over-reacting to the Soviet build up in Cuba and&amp;nbsp;that the&amp;nbsp;missile&amp;nbsp;issue should be decided at the UN,&amp;nbsp;which should also oversee their removal. Canada maintained full diplomatic relations with Castro's regime and the ambassador in Havana was advising Ottawa that the Cubans were taking the threat of an American invasion very seriously.&amp;nbsp;Also Canada was not part of the Organization of American States and so had no direct involvement with US diplomatic efforts to secure legitimacy for future actions in that particular forum.&amp;nbsp;To Diefenbaker, Kennedy's actions were needlessly provocative, excessive and nothing less than super-power bullying of a small nation. The difference in the world view&amp;nbsp;of the&amp;nbsp;conservative prairie lawyer turned PM and the Ivy League author cum President was vast.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Whether Diefenbaker believed it or not,&amp;nbsp;there was&amp;nbsp;probably an existential&amp;nbsp;Soviet submarine threat to the Eastern Seaboard and the armed forces of two close allies will try to cooperate as a single&amp;nbsp;unit to defend North America.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;You are Commander, Anti-Submarine Warfare&amp;nbsp;Atlantic Fleet (COMASWLANT), now designated Commander Task Force-83 and&amp;nbsp;based at Norfolk Naval Base. Your&amp;nbsp;mission will be to locate, track and if necessary prevent Soviet submarines from closing the US east coast. Most of the escort ships and long-ranged aviation that&amp;nbsp;are usually available have been allocated to&amp;nbsp;or reserved for ongoing operations against Cuba as the Navy prepares contingency plans to blockade the island.&amp;nbsp;The possibility of&amp;nbsp;escalation to air strikes against the Soviet nuclear-armed missiles discovered there is very real. Therefore it is absolutely essential that the Red Fleet submarines, particularly the ballistic missile boats are located and tracked.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;It's a big ocean, resources are&amp;nbsp;limited and the stakes are high.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;What actually happened...&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Through a bit of bureaucratic sleight of hand&amp;nbsp;Canada's Flag Officer, Atlantic Coast&amp;nbsp;who was also designated as&amp;nbsp;NATO's Commander in Chief&amp;nbsp;Northwest Atlantic in the event of war, and his RCAF counterpart pulled out all of the stops to ramp up assistance to the USN&amp;nbsp;without getting formal political approval from Ottawa. There were limits to what could be done without Cabinet authorization but operations of the maritime patrol&amp;nbsp;squadrons could be increased and the&amp;nbsp;available destroyers of the 5th Canadian Escort Squadron were deployed. In addition both &lt;EM&gt;HMS Aldernay&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;HMS Alcide&lt;/EM&gt; of the Royal Navy's 6th Submarine Squadron based at Halifax to provide training for Canada's submariners patrolled for Soviet submarines.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;When the United States went to DEFCON-3 on 22 October the PM refused to increase Canadian readyness accordingly and&amp;nbsp;only after&amp;nbsp;SAC went to DEFCON-2 on the 24th did Diefenbaker relent and allow the Chiefs of the Naval and Air Staffs to match the US alert posture and recall &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Bonaventure &lt;/EM&gt;and her escorts&lt;EM&gt; &lt;/EM&gt;from deployment in the UK. At least&amp;nbsp;eleven confirmed submarine and PROBSUB contacts were&amp;nbsp;detected and tracked during the crisis. In addition to the&amp;nbsp;Soviet intelligence trawler &lt;EM&gt;Shkval&lt;/EM&gt;, tanker &lt;EM&gt;Alexandrov &lt;/EM&gt;and submarine&amp;nbsp;depot ship &lt;EM&gt;Terik&lt;/EM&gt;, the&amp;nbsp;tanker thought to&amp;nbsp;be a naval auxillary tasked with supporting submarines on forward deployments, were identified and shadowed by aircraft.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;U&gt;Scenario Designer's Notes&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;If you have read this far...&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Sanitizing was (is?) a term used to describe clearing a specific area of unfriendly submarines and of course NORLANT = North Atlantic. The scenario contains a significant amount of historicity but is not historically determinative. The naval OOB for the RCN is accurate for the period as are the assorted Argus and Neptune squadrons&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;the &lt;EM&gt;HMCS Bonaventure&lt;/EM&gt; CVBG spending most of the Crisis in the UK, even if it was on exercise rather than a port visit. As near as could be determined, the American warships of TF-83 were in the Atlantic Fleet but not engaged in the Cuban quarantine or the Med in October 1962. Unlike the real events there is a chance that actions in Cuba will trigger hostilities with the USSR but the player cannot influence the probability unless using the editor and firing the relevant event. Aircraft have been randomly assigned proficencies from Novice to Ace using a random number generator, use the OOB window to check the quality of your aircrew.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Diefenbaker's decision not to match the increasing American alert posture&amp;nbsp;is modelled by an event that will&amp;nbsp;send the CF air bases and thier landed aircraft into a new side, Friendly to the Player but not usable. Another random time event will&amp;nbsp;restore them to full Player control. Both events have random time triggers as does the event allocating the &lt;EM&gt;Bonaventure&lt;/EM&gt; CVBG to Player control.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;I was working on an alternative Cuban Missile Crisis scenario where the 35th President, Richard Milhous Nixon, learned of Soviet missiles in Cuba in October 1962.&amp;nbsp;Then Matrix Forum member Apache85 uploaded his epic "&lt;EM&gt;Seven Days in October&lt;/EM&gt;" scenario so I pushed mine onto the back-burner to avoid CMC overkill. However, I did not want to let all the research go to waste so&amp;nbsp;this situation represents my Plan "B". The following principle sources were used:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;Confrontation in the Sargasso Sea: Soviet Submarines during the Cuban Missile Crisis&lt;/EM&gt; by Commodore Jan Drent;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;Canadian Involvement in the Cuban Missile Crisis Reconsidered&lt;/EM&gt; by Peter T. Haydon;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;The US Navy&amp;nbsp;and the Cuban Missile Crisis&lt;/EM&gt; from the US Naval Academy Trident History Project;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;October Fury&lt;/EM&gt; by Peter Hutchthausen;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;EM&gt;The Submarines of October&lt;/EM&gt; from the NSA Archives;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Thanks to Apache85 for the Lua scripts to insert Mother Nature items.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;... and a number of others.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Hope you enjoy Sanitizing NORLANT.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;Christopher Comars, aka Randomizer&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
